  "image_name": "0e8061bebbb1cfac5d16105877bad49a.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure compares the brain microenvironment in a normal state with chronic neurodegeneration and stroke, emphasizing how disease changes barriers and potential routes for targeted drug delivery. In the normal brain, the blood–brain barrier and intact neurovascular basement membrane limit passive entry from blood into tissue, while the extracellular matrix (ECM) and resident microglia help maintain a tightly regulated environment and restrict diffusion.\n\nIn chronic neurodegeneration, the tissue appears altered by abnormal deposits and inflammation. The image highlights amyloid plaques, tau fibrils, activated microglia, and increased sulfated proteoglycans in the ECM. These disease-associated components are presented as possible triggers or docking sites for therapies, suggesting that pathological protein accumulation and matrix remodeling could be exploited to release or retain drugs locally.\n\nIn stroke, the main change is vascular damage and acute tissue disruption. The blood vessel wall becomes leaky, increasing permeability, while infiltrating monocytes and macrophages enter the injured region. The damaged area also shows necrotic cells, stronger inflammatory activity, altered adhesion molecules and ECM deposition, and elevated protease/MMP activity. Overall, the figure shows that brain disease both weakens normal protective barriers and creates new biochemical targets that could be used for selective treatment delivery.",
